# Build EchoGuard — Wi-Fi CSI Fall Detection Prototype\n\nYou are my senior embedded systems, RF sensing, Python, and machine learning engineer.\n\nBuild a functional, hackathon-ready prototype called **EchoGuard**, a privacy-preserving, camera-free fall detection system that uses Wi-Fi Channel State Information (CSI) to detect human movement.\n\n**Priority: Get a working, real-hardware sensing prototype as quickly as possible. Do not spend time polishing features before the sensing pipeline works.**\n\n## 1. Available Hardware\n\n- Two ESP32 development boards, at least one potentially being a Seeed Studio XIAO ESP32-S3.\n- A third ESP32 is available but has existing firmware that must NOT be erased or modified.\n- MacBook running macOS with Python available.\n- Raspberry Pi available as an optional processing device.\n- USB cables for programming and collecting data.\n\nFirst, inspect the local environment, available tools, ESP-IDF installation, and connected serial devices. Identify the exact ESP32 chips before choosing firmware targets.\n\nDo not automatically flash any device. Show me the detected devices and provide explicit commands for flashing the two selected boards.\n\n**Design for two ESP32s by default. Never require a third board or external router.**\n\n## 2. System Architecture\n\nThe system should operate completely offline after installation.\n\n- ESP32 #1: Wi-Fi transmitter\n- ESP32 #2: CSI receiver\n- MacBook: CSI processing, recording, detection, visualization, and alerts\n- Raspberry Pi: Optional alternative host, not required\n\nUse the ESP32 transmitter to send Wi-Fi packets to the receiver at a consistent rate. Extract actual CSI measurements from the receiving ESP32 and stream them over USB serial to the MacBook.\n\nPrefer the official Espressif repository:\n\nhttps://github.com/espressif/esp-csi\n\nUse its `csi_send` and `csi_recv` examples as the starting point.\n\nDo not invent unsupported firmware APIs or implement an unnecessary Wi-Fi mesh network.\n\n## 3. Firmware\n\nSet up separate transmitter and receiver firmware projects.\n\nThe transmitter should:\n- Send packets frequently enough to capture human motion.\n- Operate without internet connectivity.\n- Use an appropriate fixed Wi-Fi channel.\n- Display clear startup, connection, and packet transmission information.\n\nThe receiver should:\n- Enable Wi-Fi CSI using the supported ESP-IDF APIs.\n- Capture real CSI data and associated RSSI, timestamps, and packet metadata.\n- Stream data over serial with a consistent, documented format.\n- Avoid expensive processing inside the CSI callback.\n- Report packet reception rates and errors.\n\nPrefer the official Espressif CSI serial output format to minimize custom firmware changes.\n\nProvide accurate macOS installation, build, and flashing instructions using ESP-IDF.\n\n## 4. Python Backend\n\nCreate a local Python application with:\n\n- Automatic detection or manual selection of serial ports.\n- Reliable parsing of actual Espressif CSI output.\n- Handling of malformed packets and disconnections.\n- A bounded in-memory data buffer.\n- Raw data recording and CSV export.\n- Reconnect handling.\n- Real-time packet rate and signal diagnostics.\n\nProperly decode CSI complex values into per-subcarrier amplitudes, accounting for the actual CSI format and metadata.\n\nImplement filtering and smoothing to reduce interference.\n\nAnalyze changes over time to calculate a **motion activity score**.\n\nInitially use signal variance, baseline deviation, and temporal features rather than requiring a trained neural network.\n\nNo fabricated readings should appear in live hardware mode.\n\n## 5. Live Dashboard\n\nUse Streamlit or another lightweight local visualization framework.\n\nBuild a clean, modern dark-mode dashboard.\n\nDisplay:\n\n**A. Connection status**\n- Transmitter/receiver configuration\n- Serial connection status\n- Incoming packet rate\n- Signal quality indicators\n\n**B. Live CSI visualization**\n- Scrolling CSI subcarrier heatmap\n- Signal amplitude over time\n- Motion activity graph\n- Baseline deviation\n\n**C. Motion classification**\n- Empty room / low activity\n- Movement detected\n- Rapid movement\n- Potential fall\n- Unknown / insufficient data\n\n**D. Alerts**\n- Visible potential-fall alert\n- Countdown before a simulated caregiver notification\n- Cancel alert button\n- Event log\n\nNever imply that prototype predictions are medically validated.\n\n## 6. Self-Calibration\n\nAdd a guided calibration workflow.\n\nStep 1: Record an empty, stationary room baseline for 15–30 seconds.\n\nStep 2: Have the user walk across the sensing area.\n\nStep 3: Record standing still and ordinary sitting.\n\nStep 4: Determine initial signal variability, noise levels, and preliminary motion thresholds.\n\nThe dashboard should visualize the differences between the baseline and movement data.\n\nMake recalibration easy.\n\nPersist calibration parameters locally and associate them with the sensor configuration.\n\n## 7. Labeled Data Collection\n\nCreate a recording interface with buttons for these labels:\n\n- Empty room\n- Standing still\n- Walking\n- Sitting\n- Lying down\n- Rapid sitting\n- Simulated fall\n\nEach recording must contain timestamps, CSI measurements, metadata, and the selected label.\n\nProvide a way to replay recorded sessions through the same processing pipeline.\n\nOrganize recordings by session so that future machine learning evaluation can hold out complete sessions rather than randomly splitting individual packets.\n\n## 8. Basic Fall Detection\n\nInitially use an explainable, configurable heuristic:\n\n1. Detect a sudden increase in motion activity.\n2. Look for a substantial reduction in movement afterward.\n3. Mark the event as a potential fall if both conditions occur within configurable time windows.\n4. Require sufficient data quality before making a classification.\n\nShow which criteria triggered the alert.\n\nThis is only a baseline. Do not assume low movement necessarily means a person is on the ground.\n\nAdd optional training code for a Random Forest classifier once labeled real data exists.\n\nDo not claim accuracy without evaluating held-out real recordings.\n\n## 9. Simulation Mode\n\nBuild a separate simulation mode that works without connecting an ESP32.\n\nGenerate plausible synthetic CSI-like movement patterns for:\n- Empty room\n- Walking\n- Sitting\n- Rapid movement\n- Potential falls\n\nAllow adjustable noise, motion strength, and event duration.\n\nSimulated data must be clearly identified as synthetic and should never be used as evidence of real-world fall detection accuracy.\n\nUse the same dashboard and detection pipeline for simulation and live data.\n\nProvide a replay feature for real recorded CSI.\n\n## 10. Project Structure and Documentation\n\nOrganize the project into:\n\n- `firmware/transmitter/`\n- `firmware/receiver/`\n- `backend/`\n- `dashboard/`\n- `simulation/`\n- `data/`\n- `tests/`\n- `README.md`\n\nAdjust the structure if using official ESP-CSI examples without copying their entire repository.\n\nInclude:\n\n- `requirements.txt` or `pyproject.toml`\n- A one-command way to start the dashboard\n- Setup instructions for macOS\n- ESP32 flashing commands\n- Serial device troubleshooting\n- Basic unit tests for CSI parsing and motion detection\n\n## 11. Execution Order\n\nWork in this exact order:\n\n**Milestone 1 — Working CSI connection**\n\nGet real ESP32 CSI measurements into Python and print packet counts and amplitudes. This is the highest priority.\n\n**Milestone 2 — Live dashboard**\n\nDisplay actual CSI heatmaps and movement scores in real time.\n\n**Milestone 3 — Calibration and recording**\n\nImplement baseline calibration and labeled data collection.\n\n**Milestone 4 — Fall detection**\n\nImplement the initial heuristic classifier and test it with recorded examples.\n\n**Milestone 5 — Simulation and presentation**\n\nAdd synthetic demonstrations, event replay, visual improvements, and export functionality.\n\nEach milestone should produce an independently usable system.\n\nIf hardware is not connected, implement the software against the documented Espressif format and add fixtures and simulation support. Do not pretend hardware integration has been verified.\n\n## 12. Acceptance Criteria\n\nThe minimum successful prototype must:\n\n1. Run locally on my MacBook.\n2. Receive and visualize actual CSI packets from two ESP32s.\n3. Visibly react when someone moves between the ESP32s.\n4. Support recording and replaying labeled motion data.\n5. Offer empty-room baseline calibration.\n6. Trigger an explainable potential-fall alert from configurable signal features.\n7. Continue functioning without internet access.\n\n**Begin implementing the project now, starting with the official ESP-CSI examples and the serial-to-Python processing pipeline. Prioritize a testable prototype over perfection. Do not stop after producing a plan.**
